import Mathlib

/-!
# A310007: coordination sequence Gal.4.31.1

Source: https://oeis.org/A310007 and Brian Galebach, k-uniform tilings (k <= 6) and their
A-numbers, https://oeis.org/A250120/a250120.html (tiling #123, 4-uniform tiling 31 of 151,
standard notation [3^2.4.12; 3.4.6.4; (3.12^2)^2]).

Conjectures stated here (OEIS A310007, FORMULA section):
  Conjectures from Chai Wah Wu, Dec 10 2018: a(n) = 2*a(n-1) - 3*a(n-2) + 4*a(n-3) - 5*a(n-4) + 6*a(n-5) - 7*a(n-6) + 8*a(n-7) - 7*a(n-8) + 6*a(n-9) - 5*a(n-10) + 4*a(n-11) - 3*a(n-12) + 2*a(n-13) - a(n-14) for n > 16. G.f.: (-2*x^16 + 4*x^15 - 3*x^14 + 5*x^13 - 5*x^12 + 12*x^11 - 3*x^10 + 12*x^9 - 2*x^8 + 9*x^7 + 8*x^5 + 3*x^4 + 4*x^3 + x^2 + x + 1)/((x - 1)^2*(x^2 + 1)^2*(x^4 + 1)^2).

The tiling. Galebach gives each vertex type as its cyclic sequence of polygons together with,
for every edge, the type of the neighbour and its rotation (a prime marks a reflection):
  Gal.4.31.1: A: 12^2.3; A 240; B 180; A 120
  Gal.4.31.2: B: 12^2.3; C 180'; A 180; C 120
  Gal.4.31.3: C: 12.4.3^2; B 180'; C 120'; D 270'; C 60'
  Gal.4.31.4: D: 6.4.3.4; D 60; D 300; C 270'; C 210
All edges have length 1. Rebuilding the tiling from this notation gives a periodic graph.
Its translation lattice has basis t1 = (-2 - √3, 3 + 2√3) and t2 = (-2 - √3, -3 - 2√3).
The vertex (x, y, i) is the point x t1 + y t2 + p_i, where p_i and the vertex type are:
  i = 0: p_i = (-3 - 2√3, 0), type A
  i = 1: p_i = (-2 - √3, -1 - √3), type A
  i = 2: p_i = ((-3 - 2√3)/2, (-2 - √3)/2), type A
  i = 3: p_i = ((-3 - 2√3)/2, (6 + 3√3)/2), type A
  i = 4: p_i = (-1 - √3, -1 - √3), type A
  i = 5: p_i = (0, 0), type A
  i = 6: p_i = ((-6 - 3√3)/2, 1/2), type B
  i = 7: p_i = ((-4 - 3√3)/2, (-3 - 2√3)/2), type B
  i = 8: p_i = ((-3 - 2√3)/2, (-√3)/2), type B
  i = 9: p_i = ((-3 - 2√3)/2, (4 + 3√3)/2), type B
  i = 10: p_i = ((-2 - √3)/2, (-3 - 2√3)/2), type B
  i = 11: p_i = ((-√3)/2, 1/2), type B
  i = 12: p_i = ((-5 - 3√3)/2, (1 + √3)/2), type C
  i = 13: p_i = ((-5 - 3√3)/2, (3 + √3)/2), type C
  i = 14: p_i = ((-4 - 3√3)/2, 1/2), type C
  i = 15: p_i = ((-4 - 3√3)/2, (3 + 2√3)/2), type C
  i = 16: p_i = (-2 - √3, 0), type C
  i = 17: p_i = (-2 - √3, 2 + √3), type C
  i = 18: p_i = (-1 - √3, 0), type C
  i = 19: p_i = (-1 - √3, 2 + √3), type C
  i = 20: p_i = ((-2 - √3)/2, 1/2), type C
  i = 21: p_i = ((-2 - √3)/2, (3 + 2√3)/2), type C
  i = 22: p_i = ((-1 - √3)/2, (1 + √3)/2), type C
  i = 23: p_i = ((-1 - √3)/2, (3 + √3)/2), type C
  i = 24: p_i = ((-3 - 3√3)/2, (1 + √3)/2), type D
  i = 25: p_i = ((-3 - 3√3)/2, (3 + √3)/2), type D
  i = 26: p_i = ((-3 - 2√3)/2, (√3)/2), type D
  i = 27: p_i = ((-3 - 2√3)/2, (4 + √3)/2), type D
  i = 28: p_i = ((-3 - √3)/2, (1 + √3)/2), type D
  i = 29: p_i = ((-3 - √3)/2, (3 + √3)/2), type D
`adj i` lists the neighbours of (0, 0, i) as triples (dx, dy, j). The edge set is invariant
under the translations, so (x, y, i) is adjacent exactly to the (x + dx, y + dy, j).
From each vertex type this graph reproduces the 50 terms of the coordination sequence in
Galebach table, and from the base vertex (0, 0, 0) (type A) the data of A310007.

The graph table was generated by a script from Galebach expanded notation, with exact
coordinates in Z[ζ12].
-/

namespace OMA.S_oeis_a310007

/-- `(x, y, i)` is the vertex `i` of the fundamental cell, translated by `x t1 + y t2`. -/
abbrev Vertex := ℤ × ℤ × Fin 30

/-- The neighbours of `(0, 0, i)`, as `(dx, dy, j)`. -/
def adj : Fin 30 → List (ℤ × ℤ × Fin 30) := ![
  [(0, 0, 6), (0, 1, 3), (1, 1, 5)],
  [(0, 0, 2), (0, 0, 4), (0, 0, 7)],
  [(0, 0, 1), (0, 0, 4), (0, 0, 8)],
  [(0, -1, 0), (0, 0, 9), (1, 0, 5)],
  [(0, 0, 1), (0, 0, 2), (0, 0, 10)],
  [(-1, -1, 0), (-1, 0, 3), (0, 0, 11)],
  [(0, 0, 0), (0, 0, 12), (0, 0, 14)],
  [(0, 0, 1), (0, 1, 21), (0, 1, 23)],
  [(0, 0, 2), (0, 0, 16), (0, 0, 18)],
  [(0, 0, 3), (0, 0, 17), (0, 0, 19)],
  [(-1, 0, 13), (-1, 0, 15), (0, 0, 4)],
  [(0, 0, 5), (0, 0, 20), (0, 0, 22)],
  [(0, 0, 6), (0, 0, 13), (0, 0, 14), (0, 0, 24)],
  [(0, 0, 12), (0, 0, 15), (0, 0, 25), (1, 0, 10)],
  [(0, 0, 6), (0, 0, 12), (0, 0, 16), (0, 0, 24)],
  [(0, 0, 13), (0, 0, 17), (0, 0, 25), (1, 0, 10)],
  [(0, 0, 8), (0, 0, 14), (0, 0, 18), (0, 0, 26)],
  [(0, 0, 9), (0, 0, 15), (0, 0, 19), (0, 0, 27)],
  [(0, 0, 8), (0, 0, 16), (0, 0, 20), (0, 0, 26)],
  [(0, 0, 9), (0, 0, 17), (0, 0, 21), (0, 0, 27)],
  [(0, 0, 11), (0, 0, 18), (0, 0, 22), (0, 0, 28)],
  [(0, -1, 7), (0, 0, 19), (0, 0, 23), (0, 0, 29)],
  [(0, 0, 11), (0, 0, 20), (0, 0, 23), (0, 0, 28)],
  [(0, -1, 7), (0, 0, 21), (0, 0, 22), (0, 0, 29)],
  [(0, 0, 12), (0, 0, 14), (0, 0, 25), (0, 0, 26)],
  [(0, 0, 13), (0, 0, 15), (0, 0, 24), (0, 0, 27)],
  [(0, 0, 16), (0, 0, 18), (0, 0, 24), (0, 0, 28)],
  [(0, 0, 17), (0, 0, 19), (0, 0, 25), (0, 0, 29)],
  [(0, 0, 20), (0, 0, 22), (0, 0, 26), (0, 0, 29)],
  [(0, 0, 21), (0, 0, 23), (0, 0, 27), (0, 0, 28)]]

def neighbors (v : Vertex) : Finset Vertex :=
  ((adj v.2.2).map (fun d => (v.1 + d.1, v.2.1 + d.2.1, d.2.2))).toFinset

/-- The vertices at graph distance at most `n` from the base vertex `(0, 0, 0)`. -/
def ball : ℕ → Finset Vertex
  | 0 => {(0, 0, 0)}
  | n + 1 => ball n ∪ (ball n).biUnion neighbors

/-- The coordination sequence: the number of vertices at distance exactly `n`. -/
def a : ℕ → ℤ
  | 0 => 1
  | n + 1 => ((ball (n + 1)).card : ℤ) - (ball n).card

open PowerSeries in
theorem main :
    (∀ n : ℕ, 16 < n → a n = 2 * a (n - 1) - 3 * a (n - 2) + 4 * a (n - 3) - 5 * a (n - 4) + 6 * a (n - 5) - 7 * a (n - 6) + 8 * a (n - 7) - 7 * a (n - 8) + 6 * a (n - 9) - 5 * a (n - 10) + 4 * a (n - 11) - 3 * a (n - 12) + 2 * a (n - 13) - a (n - 14)) ∧
      mk a * ((X - 1)^2*(X^2 + 1)^2*(X^4 + 1)^2 : PowerSeries ℤ) =
        -2*X^16 + 4*X^15 - 3*X^14 + 5*X^13 - 5*X^12 + 12*X^11 - 3*X^10 + 12*X^9 - 2*X^8 + 9*X^7 + 8*X^5 + 3*X^4 + 4*X^3 + X^2 + X + 1 := by
  sorry

end OMA.S_oeis_a310007
